{
  "id": "people-on-my-street",
  "title": "The People on My Street",
  "level": "B1",
  "summary": "Who lives on your street? Meet the neighbours who make one quiet street a home.",
  "teachingMethod": "Theme-based",
  "teachingMethodReason": "Theme-based rather than story-based: one theme (the neighbours on a single street) organizes the book, and each page recycles the same structure - a person, identified by a defining relative clause, and what they do. There is no problem, no sequence of events and no resolution, so it fails the story-based test that mystery-missing-cat passes. It is not CLIL, since no subject content is taught and the closing reflection is social rather than curricular. Chosen to carry defining relative clauses with \"who\" and \"that\", a core B1 structure that no other book in the library uses.",
  "grammarFeatures": [
    "Defining relative clauses with \"who\" (the woman who lives next door)",
    "Defining relative clauses with \"that\" (the bread that we eat)",
    "Simple present for permanent states and jobs",
    "\"Want to be\" for ambitions"
  ],
  "introFunFacts": [
    "🏘️ Every page introduces one neighbour. See how many you can remember at the end!",
    "🔗 Watch for 'who' and 'that' - they join two ideas together into one longer sentence.",
    "💭 At the end, think about the people who live on YOUR street."
  ],
